elon just claimed that tesla will ship a  fully functional humanoid robot by next  year naturally the press thinks it's  impossible but could he actually do it  let's look at what's possible now boston  dynamics already has a robot that can do  parkour and 10 years ago they built a  robot that could run at 28 miles per  hour the teslabot will only go 5 miles  per hour so it doesn't seem impossible  in fact if elon wanted to release a  robot that matched the capabilities of  boston dynamics next year he could and  i'm being a hundred percent serious  boston dynamics is only worth one  billion dollars but elon is worth over  180 billion dollars so he could just buy  boston dynamics paint the atlas robot  black and start producing them next year  obviously elon isn't going to do that  but the fact that he could should make  you pause before you say that the tesla  bot will not ship next year and even if  elon misses his target ship date by  years does it really matter two things  are clearly true building the teslabot  doesn't break the laws of physics so  it's possible and building such an  advanced piece of technology will  require lots of smart people to work on  the project and that's why tesla is  recruiting so aggressively elon is known  for always announcing unrealistic  timelines and then missing them  sometimes by years but i think this is a  lot less important than most people seem  to think everyone agrees that steve jobs  was one of the most impactful  technologists of the last hundred years  but he missed deadlines all the time too  in 1983 he said that he wanted to build  a networked computer the size of a book  and would ship it by the end of the  decade apple strategy is really simple  what we want to do is we want to put an  incredibly great computer in a book that  you can carry around with you that you  can learn how to use in 20 minutes we  want to do it this decade and we really  want to do it with a radio link in it so  you don't have to hook up to anything  you're in communication with all these  larger databases and other computers  well the first ipad didn't ship until  2010 so jobs missed his prediction by a  full 20 years but it didn't matter at  all in the time between 1983 and 2010  apple shipped dozens of incredible  products that moved the entire industry  forward looking back now no one  remembers that jobs missed that deadline  just that when he finally did ship the  ipad it was a great product that steve  jobs clip comes from a talk he gave in  aspen and his goal with the talk was to  recruit talented engineers to come work  for apple elon is doing the exact same  thing with ai day he's highlighting all  of the incredible advances that tesla  has made in the field of self-driving  and teasing what's going to come next  and it's the advances in self-driving  that we should really be focusing on  because the stuff that tesla is doing  right now is absolutely incredible  the entire company has basically  transformed over the past two decades  what started as a hardware company  focused on building cars has transformed  into a software company focused on  building self-driving technology and now  the ai models that power tesla's  self-driving cars have become so complex  that tesla has gone a layer deeper and  become a hardware company that makes  computer chips it's really incredible  stuff during tesla's ai day elon's team  explained how they are tackling hard  problems across three key domains the  first is the core neural network  architecture that powers autopilot it's  a common misperception that self-driving  cars use a single ai system to process  camera data and then control the car  there are a few companies that are  trying to do that type of end-to-end  self-driving like comma ai but tesla  actually breaks the problem down into  smaller parts ai is only used to  understand the environment around the  car the actual decision making about  where to drive within that environment  is done using more traditional software  development techniques even though tesla  could use machine learning to plan out  the route taken by the car they don't  and elon had a great explanation as for  why as long as you've got like surround  video neural nets for understanding  what's going on and can convert the  surround video into vector space then  you basically have a video game if  you're in grand theft auto whatever you  can you can make the cars drive around  and pedestrians walk around without  crashing you don't have to have a neural  net for control and planning see  programmers have been able to design  basic systems for cars and video games  for years that react to environments and  avoid collisions that part is actually  not that hard the hard part is  understanding the real world and all of  its incredible variations as if it was  as simple as a video game  elon stressed the importance of surround  video and vector space because that's  where tesla took a big step forward  fortunately those two phrases sound more  complicated than they actually are  surround video just means stitching all  of the different cameras around the car  into one big image years ago when tesla  implemented the first version of  autopilot the car only had one camera so  this wasn't an issue the only job of the  autopilot was to keep you in the lane  and stop you from running into a car  directly in front of you but now tesla  cars ship with lots of cameras placed  all around the car this creates a ton  more data for the machine learning  algorithms to process and up until  recently the tesla systems were dealing  with each camera feed individually this  caused some problems though like when a  big truck appeared on multiple cameras  it became very difficult to understand  exactly where that truck was in the real  world allowing the system to see all the  cameras at once as surround video also  made it possible to translate the  two-dimensional images into  three-dimensional representations that's  what elon is talking about when he says  vector space the new autopilot system  now takes all of the camera images and  creates a fully 3d representation of the  data just like a video game and this has  some powerful implications especially on  the second key tesla innovation training  data every machine learning system needs  data and there are three main ways that  companies generally get that data the  most reliable data is hand labeled by a  human but that's very expensive using a  separate ai system to automatically  label data is much cheaper but it can be  prone to error lastly you can create an  infinite amount of data that's perfectly  labeled using simulated environments but  that has drawbacks too ai experts have  debated which kind of data is best for  years but tesla just said yes and to all  three kinds they hired a thousand data  labelers built an automated system for  labeling the data that the humans  couldn't get to in time and then also  developed a remarkably realistic  simulation environment for generating  even more data all of these systems help  teach autopilot about various situations  that it might see in the real world and  they all support each other and help  fill in gaps in the data for example the  simulated environment is great for  creating bizarre scenarios like a group  of people running down the middle of a  highway it's unlikely to happen in the  real world but if it does happen you'll  want your tesla to know what to do and  all of this is aided by that move to  vector space that elon mentioned human  labelers can now manually identify a  traffic cone in a 3d environment that  looks like a video game and the raw  images from each camera that saw that  traffic cone will be updated  automatically this three-pronged  approach shows that the autopilot team  isn't turning away any good ideas  they'll use every tool at their disposal  to build a reliable system but doing so  creates new problems tesla now has  hundreds of thousands of cars sending in  real world video feeds every single day  and most of those video feeds get  labeled either by humans or automated  systems so the ai team at tesla now has  an immense amount of data to work with  they retrain their models from start to  finish multiple times per month which  means running all of that data through a  massive supercomputer in order to  recalculate the entire neural network  this is an incredibly computationally  complex task and it has to be done on a  special type of computer chip  traditionally the training of neural  networks has been done using graphics  processing units or gpus and there's  really only one major player in the gpu  space at the moment nvidia the problem  is that nvidia has increased their  prices to the point where it no longer  makes sense for big companies like tesla  but there is another option in 2016  google released the tensor processing  unit or tpu it's a chip specifically  designed for training these machine  learning models it's a good option for  many companies but it wouldn't work for  tesla because they are in direct  competition with google see you can buy  an nvidia graphics card and basically do  whatever you want with it but google's  tpus are kept behind lock and key in  google's cloud data centers and in order  to use them you have to agree to their  terms of service the thing is one of the  clauses in that agreement states that  you promise not to use their systems to  train self-driving cars so that option  is off the table for tesla but elon saw  this coming and had the tesla team start  building their own d1 chips that would  serve as the basis for a new  supercomputer called dojo that could be  used to train autopilot they still  haven't deployed this system fully but  for anyone who is paying close attention  this was by far the biggest announcement  of the day the scale of everything that  they are doing is absolutely insane a  single d1 chip is a thousand times more  powerful than a brand new ps5 and tesla  has connected one million of these chips  together to create their compute center  the complete system has 1.1 extra flops  of computing power which is so massive  it's likely the most powerful  supercomputer ever created the  combination of improvements to the  neural network training data and  underlying hardware are going to  compound to help tesla finally get  across the finish line and deliver full  self-driving but elon obviously isn't  stopping there after over an hour of  technical demonstrations elon decided to  have some fun and spent five minutes  talking about the teslabot he's clearly  having fun with it but i do think he  really believes that tesla will be able  to transfer a lot of the work that  they've done in self-driving to the  humanoid form but there's a big question  why try to make it look so much like a  person science fiction authors have been  writing about humanoid robots for  decades but building something with all  the flexibility of a human body is  incredibly difficult after all it's much  easier to build a robot vacuum than an  actual robot that vacuums i think the  humanoid form could actually wind up  being an intermediate step on the path  to even more useful robots kind of like  how apple tried to make everything look  analog in the first versions of the  iphone consider the calculator app in  the 1980s running calculations on a  computer required keying in values on  the command line the device looked  nothing like a real world calculator but  when apple released the iphone they  opted for a skeuomorphic design that  mimicked physical calculators only after  a few years did apple realize that users  didn't need this familiarity and so they  opted for a more minimal design the same  could be true for robotics we could move  from irobot vacuum cleaners to teslabots  and then to something entirely new we  don't quite know but i'm excited to see  where it goes and remember even if elon  delivered the teslabot in the year 2040  he would still be faster at shipping  than steve jobs and that would be pretty  incredible thanks for watching

